WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
BBC Sounds, music, radio, podcasts.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Hello, I'm Brian Cox. I'm Robert Inks, and this is the Infinite Monkey Cage, and it is Christmas Day!

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Happy Christmas!

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Here's a shiny shilling, Brian Cox. Get me the finest turkey in the window.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Hope you got everything you wanted under the tree.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Brian didn't, unsurprisingly, because Brian got a particle accelerator, and he's got one of those already.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:14.000
So I was like, I wanted a Hubble Space Telescope. No one understands me. I'm going back to my room to listen to Heaven 17.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Heaven 17?

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Yeah, I thought Heaven 17 was quite a good choice for you. I thought you'd been into them.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.000
It was either them or Flock of Seagulls, but Human League was kind of too mainstream, I thought.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:35.000
So today, we're going to visit Father Christmas' home and see if we can find Santa and discover why Brian didn't get the presents that he demanded.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:43.000
As usual, Robin is in a linear superposition of right and wrong, because we are going to the North Pole today, but not in search of Santa.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Though we will have a little look, won't we?

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Maybe. But mainly, we'll be looking at how our proximity to the geomagnetic North Pole affects navigation.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.000
What does the latest research tell us about the Northern Lights and the behaviour of our star?

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:03.000
And how is the polar environment changing, and what effect is that having on the flora and fauna of the far North?

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.000
And also, what is the best way of hosting a cocktail party at the North Pole?

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Now, astonishingly, that last statement is part of the not wrong branch of Robin's wave function.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:26.000
To help us explore, we are joined by an astrophysicist arctic explorer, a physiologist marine biologist, and a comedian with an antipathy to the aurora borealis, for some reason.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.000
And they are...

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.000
My name is Felicity Aston. I'm a polar explorer.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.000
And the coldest temperature I've ever experienced was not at the North Pole. I've been there several times.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:43.000
It was actually in a place called the Pole of Cold, which is the coldest inhabited place in the world.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.000
This is a tiny village called Omikon in the very northeast of Siberia.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:55.000
And there's a population of about 300 people there, but their average wintertime temperature is below minus 70 degrees centigrade.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Oh my goodness.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.000
Yeah.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Hi, I'm Lloyd Peck. I'm the physiologist marine biologist, and I'm a polar one.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.000
I work for the British Antarctic Survey, and my thing is scuba diving underneath ice.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.000
And the best way I've found in the past to keep warm when you're cold is to get in a sleeping bag with somebody else.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.000
And body heat is a really good way of keeping warm.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Another story.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.000
I'm Russell Kane. I'm a comedian. If you're over 50 and your name's Terry, you can put it in inverted commas.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:34.000
And the coldest I've ever been was in Luton.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.000
It was last December, just after Keir Starmer took my nan's radiator allowance away.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Well, that's ended a rather gloomy opening to us, hasn't it?

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Now, I wish we'd done the Pole of Cold now. That seems upbeat, doesn't it?

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Anyway, this is our panel.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Well, I suppose, really, to start off, Lucy, with you, one of the major questions a lot of people at home will be thinking about at the moment is

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:10.000
how difficult is it to build an enormous toy factory in an area like the North Pole?

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.000
I mean, what would be the issues?

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:23.000
I've been to the North Pole a number of times, and I go quite often into schools to talk about the Arctic and the North Pole and all these sorts of things.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:31.000
And the question I always get asked, without fail, is have you ever seen Father Christmas or any elves at the North Pole?

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:36.000
And you can hear the collective intake of breath from all the adults in the room.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Like, you do not mess with Father Christmas.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.000
And you can hear them thinking, what's she going to say? What's she going to say? What secrets is she going to give away?

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:51.000
But unfortunately, I have never seen any sign of Father Christmas or the elves at the North Pole, because it's magic, isn't it?

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.000
You're not supposed to see it.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:58.000
But I have seen Father Christmas, but he's on the Arctic Circle in his office.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:05.000
There is an office in Finland, just north of Rovaniemi, and Father Christmas is there pretty much every day with his elves.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.000
And I have been to see him many times, not always accompanied by children.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.000
So that is the place to go to see Father Christmas, not the North Pole.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.000
So that is what I can reveal.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.000
How do we define the North Pole? How is it defined?

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Depends which North Pole you are talking about, because there are many North Poles on planet Earth.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:32.000
The two that are absolute North Poles by anybody's definition is the Magnetic North Pole and the Geographic North Pole.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:38.000
The Geographic North Pole is the one that everybody thinks of, the one at the top of our planet at 90 degrees north.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:46.000
The Magnetic North Pole is the North Pole of our magnetic field that surrounds planet Earth.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.000
So that's not too far away from the Geographic North Pole.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.000
But then it starts to get a little bit more esoteric.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Some people think these are true North Poles, some people think it's a load of rubbish.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.000
The Geomagnetic North Pole is like a theoretical North Pole.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:12.000
This is if the Earth really was a definite bar magnet and we had a perfect magnetic field that was all nice and even and uniform,

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.000
then that's where our North Pole would be.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.000
So this is the North Pole that's used by a lot of computer models.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.000
But then you've got things like the Pole of Cold that I just mentioned.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.000
And some people go, that's not really a proper North Pole.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:34.000
And you've got the Pole of Inaccessibility, which is the place that is most inaccessible from all directions.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:39.000
And you've got a Northern Pole of inaccessibility and a Southern Pole of inaccessibility.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.000
And then you really do start to get a little bit bottom of the barrel.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.000
So there's the Celestial North Pole.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.000
That's not the bottom of the barrel!

00:06:53.000 --> 00:07:00.000
Astronomers use this a lot because if you imagine that the sky is actually a sphere that surrounds the Earth,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:07.000
then the North Pole of that celestial sphere, if you project that down onto the surface of the Earth,

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:11.000
then some people say, well, that's another North Pole on the surface of the planet.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:14.000
So how many is that? Is that six? That's six.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:19.000
So, you know, you could maybe stretch it to seven if you include the Pole of Cold.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:25.000
So it's two kind of North Poles and then just some general flibbity-jibbity stuff.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.000
You could go for three at least.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.000
Do you reckon three?

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.000
I reckon you could go for three absolutely rock-solid ones.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.000
Which are?

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.000
What's the third one then you go for?

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.000
So there's the Geographic, there's the Magnetic, and there's the Geomagnetic.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.000
Oh, fair enough, yeah.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.000
Those three you can measure. The others are subject to argument.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.000
And so the Geomagnetic Pole is the one that's wandering.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.000
The Geomagnetic Pole is fixed, it's the Magnetic one that's wandering.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.000
So that one's wandering around.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.000
And that one sometimes flips.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.000
So hang on, right, look, this is going to be an absolute.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:03.000
I'll tell you what, considering most of the people listening at home are drunk already on Christmas Day,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.000
this is not following Christian values.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.000
So where is the Magnetic North Pole now?

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:16.000
Well, for ages it was sort of floating around the Queen Elizabeth Islands in northern Canada,

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:19.000
Northwest Territories and Nunavut.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:23.000
But then the last ten years it started really accelerating,

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:28.000
so it started moving 50 to 70 kilometres in a year.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.000
So it's gone right the way across the Arctic Ocean,

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:35.000
it's now closer to the coast of Siberia than it is to the coast of Canada.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.000
It'll never go south though, will it?

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.000
Like it won't go on tour and appear in Manchester or something.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.000
It will.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.000
Yeah, it will.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.000
But it'll come here.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:44.000
Well, it'll go down to the South Pole.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.000
But it doesn't actually, it doesn't keep, let's just get this clear,

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:50.000
it doesn't kind of keep sliding down, does it?

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.000
There's a point, isn't there, when it flips.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.000
So it doesn't kind of just go, oh, it's just gone past Guernsey.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.000
I'm not sure.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.000
It doesn't do that, does it?

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.000
No, it's a bit like the expansion of the Big Bang.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.000
You know, there's definite theories,

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.000
but no one yet knows which of those theories are right.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:11.000
So one theory is that our magnetic field will slowly decrease until there's nothing,

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:17.000
and then it will reappear equally gradually over time in a different orientation.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:20.000
Another theory is that it's an instant flip

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:23.000
and that we might get lots of flips really rapidly.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:26.000
But, I mean, we're talking rapidly over geological time.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.000
We have a record of it, don't we?

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.000
Because we have essentially a barcode across the Atlantic, for example,

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:36.000
when rocks cool and they line up with the magnetic field.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.000
So we can see when it flips in the past.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.000
And it's quite, it's irregular, isn't it?

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:45.000
We should say this is the North Pole to which your compass points.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.000
And then the question arises, as an explorer, is that a problem?

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:50.000
Yeah.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:51.000
Your compass is pointing to different places.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.000
Yeah, okay.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:54.000
So the last time I skied to the North Pole,

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.000
a helicopter set me down on the frozen surface of the Arctic Ocean

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.000
about 80 kilometres away from the North Pole.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.000
And I have to find my way to the North Pole.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.000
There's nothing to see out there.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.000
So every direction looks exactly the same.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:10.000
So what do you do?

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.000
Do you use a compass?

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:16.000
But the problem with a compass is that it's pointing not to the geographic North Pole

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.000
that you're aiming towards.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.000
It's pointing towards the magnetic North Pole,

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:24.000
which is several hundred kilometres away from the geographic North Pole.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:27.000
And occasionally in Canada and occasionally in Siberia.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:28.000
Exactly.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.000
So you can't really use a compass.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:37.000
And the other difficulty is that the difference between the magnetic North Pole

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.000
and the geographic North Pole changes depending on your location.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.000
It's called declination.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.000
So you have to know what the declination is

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:51.000
so that you can use a compass to direct you to the geographic North Pole.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:55.000
And you're trying to do that while the ice that you're standing on is also moving

00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:00.000
in an erratic motion, not always at the same speed or in the same direction.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.000
So then you think, well, I'll use a GPS then.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.000
So, yeah, okay, you can use a GPS.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:09.000
But you're at temperatures of maybe minus 40 degrees centigrade.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:15.000
And every handheld GPS device that there is uses batteries of some description.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:18.000
And they run down very quickly at those sorts of temperatures.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.000
So you can't always navigate using a GPS.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:25.000
So unless you're going to drag out a sextant, which, you know,

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:27.000
they used to use in the old days.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.000
But that's working out your position based on the position of the sun.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:34.000
A bit difficult when it's really cloudy and you can't see the sun.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:39.000
So all of these different methods of finding your way to the North Pole

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.000
have their inherent disadvantages in them.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:45.000
And so you end up sort of using a bit of a mishmash of all of it.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:48.000
So you go, oh, we'll just go to the right a little bit.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:52.000
And then maybe the ice will drift us back on to the North Pole and all will be good.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.000
So it's all a bit of a guessing game.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:57.000
I can't get anywhere without a sat-nav.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:58.000
I'll just be dead within.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.000
How did you get there?

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.000
I mean, it explains why I've never seen Father Christmas, doesn't it?

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.000
I mean, maybe I've never actually been to the North Pole

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.000
and it's all just been a big mistake.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:11.000
Lloyd, you make it even more difficult for yourself by diving under it.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:15.000
Could you actually outline what the challenges are

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:19.000
in diving in the Arctic Ocean and in Antarctica?

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:22.000
So in one sense, we don't have it more difficult

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:25.000
because we usually know where we are.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:27.000
And we've got batteries in our GPS system,

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:31.000
so we're pretty good because we're not out for days and weeks on our own.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:33.000
So our location is pretty good.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:38.000
The way we dive through ice is we take out a five-foot chain saw

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:41.000
and we cut oxo patterns in the ice until we get a block.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:45.000
And the problem with that is that as you cut lines with your five-foot chainsaw,

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.000
it freezes behind the chainsaw.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:51.000
So it probably takes you about three hours to dig a hole in the ice.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.000
And you're looking aghast.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.000
Yeah, good one for the council.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.000
The pothole rate is about the same.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:59.000
And then you bounce the blocks in the middle out

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:01.000
and you pull them out with a skidoo

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.000
and you're left with a hole that's about...

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.000
And you think, I'm going to dive in that.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.000
Well, yeah.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:10.000
Not only that, you think, I'm going to dive in that because that's great.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:13.000
That's the best diving you are ever going to do on the planet anyway.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.000
What is it about that diving?

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.000
So there are several things.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:20.000
One is that that time of year when the sea is frozen,

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:22.000
everything settles out of the ocean

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:25.000
and the visibility is hundreds of metres.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:26.000
Wow.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:28.000
And you can see all this life that's around you on the seabed

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:30.000
that you wouldn't otherwise be able to see

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.000
because in the summertime when the phytoplankton blooms are on,

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.000
you can't see your hand at the end of your arm,

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:36.000
so you don't see anything.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:38.000
But you said the hole freezes over.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:39.000
So the hole does freeze over.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:42.000
So if we want to go back to the same place day after day after day,

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:44.000
we put a cover over it.

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.000
What's the cover made of then?

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:47.000
Everything else bloody freezes.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.000
Well, we use plywood.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.000
LAUGHTER

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.000
Was anyone else expecting something more scientific?

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.000
LAUGHTER

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:59.000
Because plywood's easy to get out and easy to retrieve

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:00.000
and it's flexible enough.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.000
It doesn't break like plastic does when it's cold.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.000
You said that you see the life in the winter

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:06.000
when the visibility's good.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.000
Can you describe the kinds of life you see

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.000
and the differences between the North and South Poles?

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:13.000
One of the great things that gets me excited

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.000
about life in the polar regions

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.000
is that some of it gets really big.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:21.000
So, and I did bring a couple of examples...

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:22.000
Yes!

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:24.000
..that I have in my box.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.000
We all know wood lice, yeah?

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.000
That's a group of animals called isopods.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:31.000
Well, in the oceans, there are lots of isopods

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:34.000
and they get bigger as it gets colder.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:37.000
And this is one that you would find in the Arctic.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:41.000
It looks like something from Star Wars that would kill you.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:44.000
You should edit this so it's the end of the show

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.000
and everyone goes, and this we found,

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.000
everyone went, ooh, and then it just stops.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:49.000
LAUGHTER

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.000
And that's the end. A scream.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:52.000
It's alive!

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:54.000
It's gone into my mouth!

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:56.000
LAUGHTER

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:57.000
So is that...

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:00.000
If a lobster and a wood louse had too much Sambuca

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.000
to put it on, that would be their kid.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:05.000
So they get up to 10cm, four inches long.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:07.000
They are hundreds of times bigger

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.000
than the biggest isopods that we have around our coasts.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:14.000
And Felicity, earlier, didn't you say that they jumped at you?

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:18.000
Yeah, so when I was stationed at Rothera Research Station,

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.000
which is a British Antarctic Survey research base

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:23.000
on the Antarctic Peninsula,

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:25.000
there was a marine biology building

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:27.000
that had these big aquariums.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:31.000
And part of the wintering job that you had to do

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:33.000
was that you had to do night watch.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:36.000
One of the jobs that we had to do was sent to us by Lloyd.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:38.000
He was like, you need to check my aquariums

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.000
to make sure that, you know, all the little beasties

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.000
that we've collected are all doing all right.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:46.000
So middle of the night, I've been on my own all night in the dark

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:48.000
in an Antarctic research station

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:51.000
looking like a 70s horror movie set.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:54.000
And so I let myself into this dark aquarium lab

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:58.000
and it's all dark, but apart from the red floodlights.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.000
So it looks like that scene from Alien, right?

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.000
Where there's something scuttling underneath all the lab desks.

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:05.000
And I noticed that one of the covers

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:07.000
that were over the top of the aquarium

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:09.000
had been left partly off.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:12.000
And the reason those covers were on the aquarium

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.000
is because those little woodlouse creatures

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:19.000
like to jump out of the tanks to anyone that's passing by.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:22.000
And if they land on you, they stick to you.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.000
You can't get them off very easily.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:27.000
So I was seeing that, I was like, oh no,

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:29.000
I've got to go and put that cover back on the aquarium

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.000
and these things are going to jump out at me in the dark

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:33.000
in this red light.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:34.000
But I managed to get them on

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.000
and I didn't get attacked by a woodlouse.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:37.000
I've got a question.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:39.000
Does Imodium freeze?

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:43.000
Because I would need a lot of it to survive that scenario.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:45.000
Oh no, you wouldn't.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:46.000
Honestly, it would be...

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:47.000
Who was that guy, do you remember,

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.000
when we went to the Adventurers Club?

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:50.000
The Explorers Club.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:51.000
The Explorers Club, that was it.

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.000
And he, when he got trapped underground,

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:55.000
realised that because sometimes dog excrement,

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.000
you could actually turn into a pic

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:58.000
because it would freeze hard.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:01.000
So he did a big poo and then chipped his way out

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:03.000
with his own excrement.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:06.000
And he also lost a leg and ate some of his own leg.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:08.000
I'll stick with Imodium, it sounds more convenient.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.000
So to come back to the large animals,

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:14.000
the real big story here is not the isopods.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:15.000
It's these guys.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:17.000
So, listeners, it looks...

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:20.000
It's like a star-shaped, spider-esque,

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:21.000
daddy-long-leg thing,

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:23.000
but it's the size of two adult hands.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:24.000
Very thin legs.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:26.000
It's a massive spider.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:30.000
Sorry to be pedantic, but how many legs does it have?

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.000
It looks more like a living asterisk,

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:33.000
so you can all count, can't you?

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.000
It's got ten legs.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:36.000
So, taxonomically, is it a spider?

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:37.000
No, taxonomically...

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:39.000
Indeed, indeed, Poirot.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:41.000
However, there's a big story to this as well.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:46.000
95% of sea spiders in the world have eight legs.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:48.000
But in the Antarctic, you get ten-legged species

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:50.000
and 12-legged species.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.000
And the biggest ones are three times as big as this.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:56.000
They're 60 to 70 centimetres across,

00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:00.000
and they are 5,000 times bigger than the biggest sea spiders

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.000
that we get in temperate latitudes.

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:04.000
Not poisonous, not bitey?

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:06.000
Well, they move very slowly,

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.000
and if you were to put your hand underneath its mouth

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.000
and hold it there for three or four hours,

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:13.000
you might get bitten.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:15.000
You've done that, haven't you?

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:19.000
Week five of the expedition, I'm going to do it.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:21.000
So, why...

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:24.000
Because many people have heard of island gigantism.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:27.000
What is it about the ocean, the Arctic oceans,

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:29.000
that leads to this size?

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:32.000
So, the polar oceans are different in many ways

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:35.000
from the oceans elsewhere in the world.

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:37.000
If you go into the high polar oceans,

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:40.000
the sea temperature is permanently below zero degrees.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:43.000
And that has extra characteristics.

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:47.000
One is, the amount of oxygen you can squeeze into water

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:50.000
increases as you cool it down.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.000
And that's, in simple terms,

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:55.000
it's because the molecules in the water move more slowly,

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:59.000
the gaps get bigger, and you can force more in between the gaps.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:02.000
And there's nearly twice as much oxygen in seawater

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:05.000
at the polar regions as the tropics.

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:09.000
So, one, more oxygen to feed the tissues.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:12.000
Two, these are all cold-blooded animals,

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:16.000
and that means their body temperatures are the same as the environment.

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:19.000
And, because their body temperatures are below zero,

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:22.000
their respiration rates, their amount of energy they need

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:24.000
to keep their bodies going,

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:28.000
is 25 to 30 times less than at the tropics.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:31.000
So, there's more oxygen, twice as much.

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:34.000
25 times slower rate of use.

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:36.000
Two times 25 is 50.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:40.000
You have a massive increased path length for supplying your tissues,

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:42.000
which means you can grow more tissues.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:46.000
You mentioned just that the body temperature is the same temperature as the ocean,

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:48.000
as the water, and you said below freezing.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:49.000
Yeah.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.000
So, why doesn't it freeze?

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:55.000
So, this is one of my lectures to undergraduates that takes about an hour.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:58.000
So, if you're all sitting comfortably.

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:01.000
Well done, Brian.

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:05.000
So, if you look at living cells,

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:07.000
they have to stay liquid.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:10.000
And, there are various ways that you can keep that cytoplasm,

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.000
that cellular content liquid.

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:14.000
One is you can use antifreezes.

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:18.000
And, fish in the polar regions produce protein antifreezes and glycoprotein antifreezes.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:21.000
So, they make proteins that basically, when an ice crystal starts to form,

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:24.000
they stick to the front of the ice crystal like cling film

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.000
and stop more water coming on.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:28.000
Then, they can use a whole range of things,

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:32.000
things like glycerol and things like glucose,

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:35.000
to change the point at which that liquid would freeze.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:39.000
In the same way that we can put salt into water and lower the freezing point.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:40.000
Vodka in the freezer.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:41.000
Vodka in the freezer.

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:42.000
Yeah, yeah.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:43.000
So, if you drink a lot of vodka,

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:46.000
you'd have to go to a lower temperature before you froze,

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:50.000
which I guess a lot of people in Newcastle on a Saturday night do, isn't it?

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.000
So, you can do all of that.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:56.000
But, then they can also control the amount of water in their cells.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:59.000
And, when it comes to wintertime in the Arctic,

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:08.000
the different ways that animals survive the cold is absolutely mind-boggling.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:12.000
If you go and look in Canada at the red bark beetle, it has larvae.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:17.000
That starts off using glycerol and it goes down to about minus 20 with glycerol.

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:19.000
Then, it pumps the water out of its cells,

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:22.000
so there's less chance of water molecules sticking together.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:24.000
It gets down below minus 40.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:28.000
And then, when it gets down below minus 40 towards minus 50,

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:29.000
it vitrifies.

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:34.000
And, the problem with freezing is the ice crystals break your cells open and kill you.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:38.000
But, if you vitrify, you turn into glass and there are no ice crystals.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:43.000
And, those bark beetle larvae have been taken down to minus 150.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:44.000
Wow.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:45.000
And, survive.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:46.000
Can I ask a question?

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:49.000
Is it ageing still at this point or is it in stasis?

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:53.000
So, if it was to stay frozen and it was to defrost 300 years later,

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:55.000
would it be the same age as when it froze?

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:58.000
That's a really, really, really good area of research.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.000
It is in stasis.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.000
And, is it possible we could ever use this technology to stop human ageing

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:07.000
so we could travel to distant stars and things like that?

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:10.000
So, cryogenesis, yes, that is possible.

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:15.000
In the 1950s, there was a lot of work done on freezing mammals.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:20.000
And, they were able to freeze mice and they couldn't do it with rabbits.

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:23.000
And, the problem is not with freezing them.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:24.000
The problem is warming them up.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:28.000
Because, if everything does not warm up at the same rate

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:31.000
and something goes liquid while something else is solid,

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:33.000
your animal is not going to function.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:37.000
And, that was the research that led to the invention of microwave ovens.

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:38.000
Wow.

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:40.000
So, they microwave them to warm them up?

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:45.000
I could have told them that wasn't going to work.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:46.000
Where's Nan?

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:47.000
She's on defrost.

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:49.000
She'll be 10 minutes.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:53.000
This has changed a lot of people's Christmas lunch.

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:54.000
Absolutely fascinating.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:57.000
We've spoken about the life that we see underwater.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:23:00.000
So, in terms of the life that you've seen on your expeditions,

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.000
what's the variety of animals that you see on the surface?

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:04.000
Yeah.

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:06.000
I mean, this is the wonderful thing about going to the North Pole

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:08.000
versus going to the South Pole.

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:11.000
And, one of the defining characteristics of the Antarctic Plateau

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:13.000
in the center of Antarctica is the absence of life.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:15.000
I mean, there's no wildlife there.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:18.000
There's no penguins or seals or birds or anything like that.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:23.000
But, when you are up in the North, you are surrounded by life,

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:26.000
even if you're right up there in the middle of the Arctic Ocean.

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:31.000
And, it's not just that you see it, it's that you can feel it all around you

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:35.000
from the presence of the big predators, the polar bears,

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:38.000
right down to when a big chunk of ice turns over

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:43.000
and there's lots of algae and even fish using the algae.

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:48.000
I remember being startled by a lemming in my tent when I was about...

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:50.000
That'd make you fall off a cliff, didn't it?

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:53.000
That'd be the most ironic day in history.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:57.000
And, you're thinking, how does this tiny little thing, you know,

00:23:57.000 --> 00:24:00.000
manage to survive all the way out here?

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.000
It's really incredible.

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:04.000
Everyone has a polar bear story.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:06.000
Surely, every explorer has a polar bear story.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.000
I'm glad you narrowed that down.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:10.000
Everyone has... No, actually, I shouldn't...

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:13.000
I just wondered, in terms of jeopardy,

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:15.000
you talked about big predators.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:18.000
So, what's your big predator story?

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:20.000
Oh, gosh.

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:23.000
The closest encounter I've had with a polar bear,

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:26.000
luckily, I was on a snow machine.

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:30.000
So, I was driving by myself on a snow machine

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.000
past a big area of ice...

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:35.000
So, that's a machine that goes on snow, not a machine that makes snow.

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:38.000
Exactly. A snowmobile, a skidoo, whatever you want to call it.

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:40.000
And I was keeping an eye on the ice rubble

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:43.000
because that's where the polar bears kind of like to hang out.

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:46.000
And I came over a rise to find one

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:49.000
sort of stood pretty much right in front of me.

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:53.000
I was close enough to really see the sort of definition of its muscles.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:56.000
I mean, these are frightening animals.

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:58.000
And the number of people that said,

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:00.000
oh, I really want to see a polar bear.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.000
It's like, you really don't.

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:06.000
And so, luckily, I was on a snowmobile

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:09.000
and I knew that I was in an area

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:13.000
where the locals hunted them on snow machines.

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:16.000
So, I was able just to rev the engine a bit

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:20.000
and it got up on its back legs, took a big sniff.

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:23.000
And, you know, my heart was sort of in my mouth as it's doing this

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:25.000
and then it just took off and ran.

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:28.000
So, I didn't have to even get the rifle off my back.

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:30.000
It was a bit warm after that encounter, wasn't it?

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.000
If you go and work in Svalbard

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:34.000
and you go to the UK station in Svalbard,

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:37.000
the first thing they do on the first day is

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:40.000
they give you a .375 rifle.

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:45.000
And that has bullets that are bigger than anything you've seen in a rifle before.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:47.000
They train you how to shoot it

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:49.000
and they give you thunder flashes

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:52.000
so that if the polar bear starts coming towards you,

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:55.000
you're supposed to throw the thunder flashes between you and the polar bear

00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:57.000
and the bangs make it go away.

00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:00.000
And then they say, if that doesn't happen,

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:04.000
then you have a rifle if you can't get away from it in time.

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:07.000
And, if it worse comes to worse,

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:09.000
you have to shoot the polar bear.

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:12.000
Now, you're on Norwegian territory

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:16.000
and the law says you are not allowed to shoot a polar bear

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:18.000
unless it is less than 10 metres away from you.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:23.000
And a full-sized polar bear does 11 metres per second.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:28.000
And then they say, the last time one of these was killed,

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:30.000
it took three bullets to kill it.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:35.000
So, the best advice is, if it starts stalking you,

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:38.000
get out of the way really quickly.

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:42.000
Yeah, so if you come across a bear when you're on an expedition,

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:46.000
the only responsible thing to do is to get yourself out of there

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:49.000
because we are intruders in their territory.

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:51.000
We are intruders in their territory.

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:54.000
My life is less valuable than the bears in that scenario,

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:56.000
is what you're telling me.

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:57.000
Yes.

00:26:57.000 --> 00:26:59.000
They are nine feet tall

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:02.000
and they are heavier than any two people that you know.

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:04.000
So, they are massive animals.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:07.000
Now, we're going to do...

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:09.000
Because you, I believe this is true,

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:12.000
you have in the Guinness Book of World Records

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:14.000
for the most polar bears shot...

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:16.000
Oh, God, no, we haven't hit that yet.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:18.000
You are in the Guinness Book of Records

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:21.000
for the northernmost cocktail party.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:25.000
Yes, that is a Guinness World Record that I currently hold.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.000
So, I was on an expedition

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:30.000
and we were right up at 78 degrees north.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:33.000
So, as you imagine, like polar explorers are in a tent

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:36.000
and all the polar gear with skis and everything else.

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:39.000
But in order for it to be considered a cocktail party

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:41.000
by the Guinness Book of Records,

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:44.000
there's a very specific set of criteria that you have to follow.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:46.000
So, there has to be at least five people.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:48.000
Tick, we were a five-person team.

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:51.000
You have to be in proper cocktail attire.

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:56.000
So, that is a dress for women.

00:27:56.000 --> 00:27:58.000
I mean, you know, the Guinness World Records

00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:00.000
have got something to answer about sort of...

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:03.000
And if you're a man, it's a proper lounge suit

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:05.000
or a lounge dress, cocktail dress.

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:07.000
You have to have music playing.

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:10.000
You have to have a table with a tablecloth.

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:13.000
I'm already thinking most of the parties I've ever been to in my life

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:16.000
are already not considered a cocktail party by this.

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:19.000
You have to have at least three different types of cocktails

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:22.000
and you have to have two different types of canapé,

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:25.000
one sweet and one savoury.

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:29.000
Oh, and it has to last for at least 15 minutes.

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:31.000
We had to sort of drink it quickly

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:34.000
before it froze into a sort of icicle in the bottom of the glass.

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:36.000
God, it goes straight to your head.

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:38.000
How did you defrost the canapés?

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:41.000
Oh, I knew you were going to ask that.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:43.000
So the cocktails were ready-mixed

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:46.000
and we took them in, like, these little packages.

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:50.000
We had to defrost those by sort of putting them down our fronts.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:51.000
I knew it.

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:53.000
The warmest part.

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:56.000
So, you know, under your armpit is one of the warmest pits

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:59.000
and down your pants, whatever.

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:02.000
But they were all enclosed, so that was fine.

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:04.000
But the canapés, yeah, our canapés,

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:07.000
we had to get inventive using the rations that we had.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:12.000
We are going to now try and create aurora-based cocktails

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:13.000
using a UV light.

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:18.000
So what we've got in these glasses here is a gin and tonic.

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:21.000
Just to say, because I arrived quite late for this recording,

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:23.000
I have no idea what's going on.

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:24.000
Oh, neither do I.

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:29.000
Is there some context about why you've got a UV light and some gin?

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:32.000
Do you know what, Brian, you've never complained before

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:34.000
when we found reasons that we can have alcohol in

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:36.000
which appear to be tax-deductible.

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:38.000
No, I have a vague memory of something now

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:40.000
in some production meeting a while ago.

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:41.000
So this is...

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:43.000
Something about the northern lights.

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:47.000
So that there, using a small amount of blue liqueur-style drink

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:49.000
and a Barocca and a UV light,

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:52.000
all of those things you probably receive for Christmas.

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:55.000
So just to recap, so this is an elaborate introduction

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:57.000
to the aurora borealis.

00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:00.000
Yeah, what is happening in the atmosphere that creates that kind of,

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.000
that's the greenness of the aurora borealis?

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:07.000
Yeah, so if we rewind a bit to what the aurora is,

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:11.000
so if you imagine the Earth is a big bar magnet

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:17.000
and we have this big magnetic field that spreads all the way around the globe

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:21.000
and that creates this sort of area of space around the planet

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:23.000
called the magnetosphere,

00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:26.000
and then you have the sun in the middle of the solar system

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:31.000
that is constantly sort of emitting this wind of charged particles.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:33.000
So it's this huge amount of charged particles

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:38.000
that are cascading from the sun outwards across the whole solar system.

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:42.000
And instead of hitting the Earth, it hits the magnetosphere.

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:44.000
And because these particles are charged,

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:49.000
they react with that magnetic field that surrounds the planet.

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:51.000
So it acts as a sort of shield.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:56.000
It protects us from the solar wind, from all this radiation.

00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:59.000
But some of those charged particles

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:03.000
sort of get trapped inside the magnetic field of the Earth.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:06.000
And if you can imagine sort of around the poles,

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:08.000
let's focus on the North Pole,

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:10.000
around the North Pole where the magnetic field lines

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:13.000
are sort of going into the top of the planet,

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:17.000
all these charged particles that are spinning around

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:19.000
all these magnetic field lines

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:23.000
are being dragged down towards the surface of the planet

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:25.000
around the North Pole.

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:28.000
And where those charged particles

00:31:28.000 --> 00:31:31.000
start getting dragged through the atmosphere,

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:35.000
they start interacting with other particles that are in the atmosphere,

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:38.000
so things like oxygen and nitrogen predominantly.

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:43.000
And so when a charged particle starts interacting with some oxygen,

00:31:44.000 --> 00:31:48.000
it will start getting really excited and emitting light as photons.

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:54.000
And we see that light as green if it's interacting with oxygen,

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:56.000
but we'll see it as different colours

00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:59.000
depending on which gas it's interacting with.

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:02.000
And there's so much a part of the Arctic,

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:05.000
of the experience, the aurora, both north and south.

00:32:05.000 --> 00:32:09.000
So they become part of the mythology in the Northern Lights,

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:11.000
part of the cultures of the Arctic.

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:12.000
Hugely.

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:15.000
The first time I saw the Northern Lights,

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.000
I knew about the science.

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:20.000
I knew how they were caused according to European science.

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.000
You know, we have the explanation.

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:27.000
And yet sitting there watching this phenomenon for the first time,

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:31.000
that seemed like the least likely explanation.

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:38.000
Magic seemed like a lot more realistic explanation of what I was seeing.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:42.000
I think it's important to say that this is a lot of people's beliefs,

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:46.000
and I in no way am sort of sneering or laughing at that

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:50.000
because the first time I saw it, that was what I saw.

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:54.000
I saw spirits moving across a bridge to another world.

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:57.000
And it's reflected in a lot of different cultures.

00:32:57.000 --> 00:33:00.000
So one of the loveliest stories I heard

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:06.000
was actually from First Nation tribes north of the Great Plains.

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:10.000
And they had a folkloric hero, a mythical hero,

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:12.000
almost like a god-like figure,

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:15.000
who, once he'd finished creating the world,

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:17.000
they believed travelled north.

00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:20.000
And so that he could send a message back to his people

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:22.000
that he hadn't forgotten them,

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:24.000
he would light a massive fire.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:26.000
And this is what the Northern Lights was.

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:28.000
It was a reflection of this massive fire.

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:31.000
And whenever I see the Northern Lights now,

00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:34.000
that story springs into mind

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:37.000
because it really does feel like you're seeing something

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:40.000
that connects you, not only to people around the world,

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:44.000
but also connects you to the solar system and everything beyond that.

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:47.000
This is created by something that's come from the sun.

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:49.000
It's really incredible.

00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:53.000
Is it a very, very different experience being in the northern Arctic,

00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:56.000
where you have all these rich cultural stories,

00:33:56.000 --> 00:33:58.000
and then the Antarctic,

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:01.000
where there's essentially nobody other than scientists?

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:03.000
Well, it is massively different

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:05.000
because the north is an ocean,

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:07.000
and the south is a massive continent,

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:10.000
a continent twice the size of Australia.

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:12.000
And it contains...

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:14.000
It's rock underneath it, yeah.

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:17.000
And there's no rock at the surface in the north.

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:18.000
It's an ocean.

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:22.000
And the Antarctic contains about three-quarters of the world's fresh water.

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:25.000
So the amount of ice in the Antarctic is absolutely unreal.

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:28.000
It's an area the size of Europe that's two miles thick.

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:31.000
I'd like to ask about the impact of climate change

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:35.000
on these areas in the Arctic and the Antarctic.

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:37.000
So what are we seeing change

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:40.000
over the years and decades that you've visited those areas?

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:43.000
So the biggest change, I think, is the loss of ice.

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:45.000
So in the Arctic in the 1970s,

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:48.000
we had 15,000 square kilometres of sea ice,

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:50.000
and now we've got five.

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:54.000
And the predictions are that by 2050, in the summer,

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:56.000
that minimum extent...

00:34:56.000 --> 00:34:59.000
and that's the minimum extent of ice in the Arctic...

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:02.000
will have gone, and it'll be open water in the Arctic,

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:05.000
so people will be able to sail ships through the Arctic.

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:07.000
You mentioned earlier the ice turns over,

00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:09.000
and it's green, and it's thick green.

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:14.000
And huge numbers of species live on the underside of that ice,

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:18.000
including the early life stages of things like polar cod.

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:22.000
And lots of organisms depend on that ice for their life cycles.

00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:25.000
So the effect on the ecosystem is massive.

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:27.000
And all the timings change,

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:31.000
and the ability of animals to raise themselves efficiently,

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:36.000
or even at all, becomes a really difficult problem.

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:39.000
And we know there are tipping points in the environment.

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:41.000
We know there are tipping points not just in the environment,

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:43.000
but in ecosystems.

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:45.000
And in the polar regions,

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:47.000
we don't know how much we can lose from an ecosystem

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:48.000
before it collapses.

00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:52.000
Felicity, how does that change in terms of your experience now,

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:55.000
and knowing this fragility, how different is it for you?

00:35:55.000 --> 00:35:57.000
Really emotionally, I suppose.

00:35:57.000 --> 00:36:01.000
It's really odd, because if you're in the middle of the Arctic Ocean,

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:04.000
you know how fragile this environment is.

00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:08.000
And yet, when you're there, seeing big blocks of ice

00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:10.000
coming up one over the other,

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:13.000
and size of apartment blocks being just tossed over,

00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:17.000
it feels anything but fragile when you're in that moment.

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:21.000
But it's not just the amount of ice being so much less.

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:25.000
It's the fact that what is there now is thinner, newer,

00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:28.000
and less stable than it's ever been before.

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:31.000
Whereas, say, in 1969,

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:34.000
when Sir Wally Herbert crossed the Arctic Ocean

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:37.000
and became the first team of people

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:40.000
to undisputedly get to the top of our planet,

00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:42.000
get to the North Pole.

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:46.000
But that journey is no longer possible,

00:36:46.000 --> 00:36:50.000
because they did it when there was lots of what we call multi-year ice.

00:36:50.000 --> 00:36:52.000
So this is the thick stuff.

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:54.000
It can be three metres or more,

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:57.000
and it's really stable, really strong,

00:36:57.000 --> 00:37:00.000
and it holds the pack ice together.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:06.000
The last time a polar explorer went from land to the North Pole

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:10.000
across the Arctic Ocean was in 2014.

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:13.000
It's not been possible to do it since then.

00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:17.000
So now it's only possible to do partial journeys to the North Pole.

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:21.000
Russell, what are your final thoughts on everything you've heard today?

00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:24.000
I always get invited on this programme,

00:37:24.000 --> 00:37:26.000
and I never know anything about the subject.

00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:28.000
I'm sort of like a student.

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:30.000
But I have actually seen the Northern Lights,

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:32.000
and I have actually been to the Arctic Circle

00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:34.000
for a project I was filming.

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:36.000
It was a sort of comedy survival programme.

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:39.000
So I've seen this majestic spectacle,

00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:43.000
and I was invited by the Sami,

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:47.000
the people in the northern part of Norway,

00:37:47.000 --> 00:37:50.000
and I went into something called a lavu,

00:37:50.000 --> 00:37:53.000
which is their conical-shaped traditional hut.

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:55.000
I was invited for dinner,

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:58.000
and in this programme I had two YouTubers with me called Rose and Rosie,

00:37:58.000 --> 00:38:02.000
and they'd been stressing all trip because they were vegetarians.

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:05.000
I don't think a word even exists in their language.

00:38:05.000 --> 00:38:10.000
We had to try to explain to these people what vegetarian was.

00:38:10.000 --> 00:38:12.000
And when we got there, obviously I eat anything

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:14.000
because I'm a moron from a council estate,

00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:17.000
and when I got there, dinner was...

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:20.000
You couldn't have done a more comically shocking snack for vegans.

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:23.000
It was a boiled reindeer's head,

00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:27.000
which was out, and everyone was happily picking the bits of meat off.

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:29.000
I mean, it just tasted like meat.

00:38:29.000 --> 00:38:31.000
It just looked... Obviously the eye holes and everything.

00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:34.000
And these girls were starting to cry and panicking.

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:36.000
The producer's like, OK...

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:38.000
And the translator said,

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:41.000
don't worry, he's got something without meat for you two.

00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:44.000
And it was a pancake made from blood.

00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:46.000
LAUGHTER

00:38:46.000 --> 00:38:48.000
It was delicious!

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:52.000
But, yeah, I've been there. It's like another planet.

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:55.000
It's hard to explain if you haven't done it.

00:38:55.000 --> 00:38:59.000
The more that we express the fascination, the beauty, the curiosity

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:03.000
and how much you can fall in love with the possibilities that are there,

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:07.000
then the more I think we strive to try and preserve what is there as well.

00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:10.000
Experiencing what the world has out there

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:15.000
is just way more intense than anything that you can get within a city.

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:19.000
And I would say the Northern Lights, the Southern Lights,

00:39:19.000 --> 00:39:22.000
that's one of a whole plethora of things and experiences

00:39:22.000 --> 00:39:24.000
that most people don't get.

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:26.000
But if you have experienced them,

00:39:26.000 --> 00:39:30.000
they become a substantial part of the way you function and the way you live.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:34.000
And it has effects that go beyond what you can get

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:36.000
by understanding what it is.

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:38.000
Well, we also asked our audience...

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:41.000
By the way, I'll just tell you, you know the guy I told you about

00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:44.000
who had one leg who did a poo and then used it as a pickaxe?

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:46.000
Peter Freuchen.

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:48.000
Anyway, so we always ask the audience a question

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:51.000
because we have the most intelligent audience of any Radio 4 show.

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:54.000
And I don't care what you say, Gardner's Question Time,

00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:57.000
I'll meet you out the back of the allotments and take you on.

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:00.000
Is that a poo knife?

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:02.000
Call that a poo knife?

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:04.000
This is a poo knife.

00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:07.000
This is a very strange remake of Crocodile Dundee.

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:10.000
That's not a poo. This is a poo.

00:40:11.000 --> 00:40:14.000
So we asked our audience,

00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:17.000
if you were going to make a snowman resembling anyone,

00:40:17.000 --> 00:40:19.000
who would it be and why?

00:40:19.000 --> 00:40:21.000
Is that the first one you got?

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:23.000
Hugh Jackman, I think this should be, shouldn't it?

00:40:23.000 --> 00:40:25.000
Do you know why?

00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:27.000
Hugh Jackman.

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:29.000
What films has he been in?

00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:31.000
Wolverine.

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:33.000
By the way, if you ever want to be a Wolverine at home,

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:35.000
just put Potato Grater Snowman on the...

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:37.000
The Greatest Snowman.

00:40:37.000 --> 00:40:41.000
Brian Cox building a snowman of himself

00:40:41.000 --> 00:40:44.000
because things can only get meta.

00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:46.000
Well done, well done.

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:48.000
I think I beat that, I beat that.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:50.000
Oh, come on then.

00:40:50.000 --> 00:40:52.000
It's not Brian Cox-based.

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:54.000
I know you like them to be based on YouTube, but this isn't.

00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:57.000
If you could build a really big one with a six-pack,

00:40:57.000 --> 00:40:59.000
it would be the Abdominal Snowman.

00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:01.000
Oh, very nice, yeah.

00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:03.000
Okay then, well, thank you very much to our wonderful panel.

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:06.000
Felicity Aston, Lloyd Peck and Russell Kane.

00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:08.000
Next week it's, well, it's 2026

00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:10.000
and we're going to be taking a bit of a break

00:41:10.000 --> 00:41:12.000
so Brian can tour the world

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:14.000
and I can watch every episode of The Professionals

00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:16.000
on daytime TV

00:41:16.000 --> 00:41:19.000
because I've always seen myself as a bit of a Martin Shorty

00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:21.000
or Lewis Collins.

00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:23.000
I think you're more Cowley, don't you?

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:26.000
A woolly jumper and a...

